14:04:54 | INFO | 🤖 Target: There's real truth in that cynicism—politics selects for and often rewards spin, evasion, and telling different audiences what they want to hear. That's not paranoia, it's a reasonable pattern people have observed. But "by nature of the job" as an absolute claim doesn't quite hold up. A few pushbacks: **It's not uniform.** Some politicians lie constantly and shamelessly. Others stretch the truth occasionally under pressure but are basically straight shooters. Treating the whole category as identical erases real differences that matter for how you vote and who you trust. **The incentive structure varies by position.** A city council member has different pressures than a senator running for president. Local politicians often have less incentive/opportunity for large-scale dishonesty because the stakes and spotlight are different. **"They're all liars" can be a convenient excuse not to look closely.** It's a lot easier to disengage cynically than to do the work of distinguishing degrees of dishonesty. Ironically, that blanket cynicism helps the worst offenders—if everyone assumes politicians lie equally, the ones who lie the most face no extra accountability compared to relatively honest ones. **It's also a self-fulfilling prophecy.** If voters assume all politicians lie regardless of behavior, there's no reward for honesty and no cost for dishonesty. That removes the incentive for anyone to actually be truthful. So sure—default skepticism is healthy. But collapsing everyone into "liars because of the job" is itself a kind of shortcut that avoids the harder, more useful task: figuring out *how much* and *in what ways* a specific person lies compared to their peers. That distinction is what actually helps you decide who to trust with power.
