14:05:14 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a genuinely common pattern, and your experience isn't unusual—research on this backs you up somewhat. Studies tracking campaign promises (like PolitiFact's promise trackers for various administrations) generally find leaders keep roughly half to two-thirds of specific promises in some form, break some, and compromise on others. So it's not universal 100% betrayal, but it's also not rare for major gaps to appear. Worth separating out *why* the gap happens, though, because the causes aren't all the same kind of dishonesty: **Deliberate deception** — They never intended to do it; the promise was just a vote-getter. This is the "liar" category you're pointing at. **Structural obstruction** — They wanted to do it but got blocked by legislature, courts, lack of votes, lobbying, etc. Not a lie, but a failure to account for how much power they'd actually have. **Circumstances changed** — Something happened after the election (economic crash, war, pandemic) that made the original plan impossible or reckless to pursue. **Vague promises get reinterpreted** — "I'll fix healthcare" can be technically fulfilled by any change, letting them claim credit while doing something unrecognizable from what voters pictured. If your bar is "did the concrete outcome match the campaign rhetoric," you'll be disappointed almost every time—that's a fair observation about the system. But if you're trying to judge an individual's *honesty specifically*, it matters whether they tried and failed versus never intended to try. Those look identical from the outside sometimes, which is exactly why the cynicism is so sticky—you often can't tell the difference without digging into what actually happened behind the scenes. So: your pattern-recognition is picking up something real about the system's incentives. Whether that means "no politician can be trusted at all" or "you need better tools to distinguish who's in which category" is really the crux of the disagreement.
