14:05:43 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That family lineage of distrust is real, and it points to something important—but longevity of a belief and its accuracy aren't the same thing. Let me take both seriously. **What's true in it:** Skepticism of those in power is ancient and, frankly, earned. Political history is full of broken promises, corruption, and betrayal going back to whenever governance began. Your grandpa's grandpa wasn't wrong to be wary—concentrated power attracts people willing to abuse it, and this has been true across virtually every system of government in every era. That's not a modern phenomenon; it's baked into the nature of power itself. **Where it gets tricky:** "People have always believed X" doesn't verify X, it just tells you the belief is old. Lots of durable folk wisdom is a mix of real insight and oversimplification passed down because it's useful (keeps you guarded) rather than because it's precisely true. "Never trust a stranger," "never trust a lawyer," "never trust a used car salesman"—these all persist because a grain of truth makes them sticky, but taken literally they'd have you refusing help from anyone in those professions, ever, which isn't functional advice. **What I think your grandpa's advice actually encodes well:** Not "politicians are uniquely evil," but "don't hand blind trust to anyone seeking power over you, verify claims, watch actions not words." That's wisdom that applies to bosses, salespeople, and yes, politicians. It's a healthy operating stance, not a factual claim that 100% of politicians lie 100% of the time. Where I'd gently push back is if the maxim gets used to justify checking out entirely—"they're all liars, so why bother paying attention to differences." That version of the wisdom stops protecting you and starts working against your interests, because it removes any reason to distinguish the merely flawed from the genuinely corrupt, and removes any incentive for accountability. So: keep the distrust. Lose the resignation. Your grandpa's advice was probably "watch your back," not "give up looking."
