14:07:16 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's actually a solid mental model—incentive structures do shape behavior at scale, and it's naive to expect a system that rewards lying to consistently produce honest outcomes. Economists and political scientists take this seriously; it's basically the logic behind "public choice theory." I'm not dismissing it. But "water finds its own level" implies something deterministic—like there's no variation, everyone converges to the same lying equilibrium. Real behavior under identical incentives isn't actually that uniform. Put a hundred people in the same job with the same pressure to cut corners, and you get a range: some cheat constantly, some never, most somewhere in between depending on personal ethics, risk tolerance, what they value besides winning (legacy, self-respect, religious conviction, fear of getting caught). Incentives shape the *distribution*, they don't erase individual variation entirely. This actually happens in plenty of professions with strong incentives to lie—salespeople, expense-report submitters, athletes offered doping advantages—and in every case you get corruption *and* holdouts, not total capitulation across the board. If incentives alone determined outcomes with no exceptions, you'd expect literally zero variation, and that's not what shows up even in controlled studies of dishonesty under pressure (behavioral economists like Dan Ariely have run these experiments—people cheat more under incentive, but a meaningful chunk still don't, even when they could get away with it). So I'd revise the metaphor: incentives are gravity, pulling everyone toward dishonesty—but gravity doesn't mean everything ends up at the same puddle. Some resist more than others, and that variation is exactly the thing worth tracking if you're trying to figure out who's actually different from the norm versus who's just better at hiding it.
