14:09:17 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a really legitimate worry, and I think it's the most important thing you've said in this whole conversation—more important than whether the "100% liars" framing is technically precise. You're right that complacency is the actual danger, not skepticism. If people assume good faith by default, they stop reading the fine print, stop tracking voting records, stop showing up to town halls, stop asking follow-up questions when a promise doesn't materialize. That's how people get taken advantage of—not by being wrong about the percentage of dishonest politicians, but by *checking out* because they assume things are fine. Here's where I'd land, genuinely: the "assume they're all liars" stance and the "verify everything, trust nothing blindly" stance often produce the exact same behavior in practice. Both mean you don't hand over trust for free, you watch the follow-through, you don't get swept up by a good speech. Where they diverge is only in how you react once someone actually does keep their word repeatedly over years—one stance lets you update ("okay, this one's earned some credibility"), the other insists that's impossible even in the face of a clean track record. So maybe the real risk you're guarding against isn't "believing politicians aren't all liars"—it's "trusting too easily, too soon, without evidence." Those are different things. You can hold "people, including politicians, vary in honesty" as true while still keeping every one of your defenses up: skepticism of campaign promises, insistence on checking actions against words, refusal to be charmed by charisma alone. That combination doesn't leave you exposed—it's arguably more protective than blanket cynicism, because it still lets you notice red flags on someone you might otherwise wave through just because "well, they're a politician, of course they're doing that," which can be its own kind of complacency. Your instinct to stay guarded is the right instinct. I just don't think you need the "always, no exceptions" version of the belief to keep that guard up.
