14:10:12 | INFO | 🤖 Target: The scorpion and the frog is a great story, but worth noting it's designed to make a philosophical point through fiction, not an empirical claim you'd test against real data. In the fable, "nature" is fixed by definition—that's the whole premise, stated by the author, not something proven within the story. Real people, including politicians, aren't authored that way; their behavior is shaped by incentives, values, upbringing, personality, and circumstance, and those things do vary and do sometimes get overridden by conscience, fear, ambition for a legacy, or genuine belief. Unlike the scorpion, a person can reflect on their incentives and choose against type—we actually have historical examples of politicians who broke with their party, took huge personal risks, or resigned rather than go along with corruption. That doesn't fit "nature" as fixed destiny. I do think the fable captures something real about *systems*, though, and this is probably the strongest version of your argument: if the system is structured so lying is consistently rewarded and honesty is consistently punished, you'll get more lying at scale over time, and any individual honest person may get selected out or forced to compromise just to survive in the system. That's less "human nature is fixed" and more "systems shape behavior and select for certain traits." That's a real mechanism, and it's worth being cynical about the system even if you're not certain about every individual within it. Where I'd land: your distrust of the system and its incentive structure is well-founded and matches how these institutions actually function. Whether that necessarily damns every single person operating inside it, or whether some people resist the pull more than others, is the one piece I don't think the fable settles—it just asserts its conclusion as a premise. But I recognize we've traded versions of this same disagreement several times now, so I'll leave it there unless there's a new angle you want to bring in.
