14:10:36 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's worth taking seriously as evidence—someone with direct experience saying it plainly carries some weight. A few things worth weighing alongside it, though: **Documentaries about scandals are self-selecting by design.** A film about political scandals is, by definition, assembling case studies of dishonesty and corruption—that's the premise of the project. It would be strange for the host to conclude "actually most of it's fine," because that's not the film he was making. It's a bit like watching a documentary on shark attacks and concluding all fish are dangerous—true within the frame, but the frame was built to feature the exceptional, dramatic cases, not the median experience. **"Basically the job description" is a punchy line, not a research finding.** People who've worked inside a system often do have real, valuable insider knowledge—but they also tend to talk in the same kind of blunt, quotable absolutes that make for good documentary soundbites. "It's basically the job" sounds more compelling on camera than "about 40% of the time, under specific pressures, officials misrepresent things to constituents." The format rewards the sharper claim regardless of precision. **One insider's frame reflects his specific experience.** Where did he work? What level of government, what era, what country? Someone burned by a specific corrupt system might reasonably generalize from that experience, the same way your grandpa generalized from his. That's real data, but it's one data point (or one person's aggregated impression), not a survey of the whole political class across all levels of government worldwide. I'd treat it as one more piece of corroborating testimony for something you already believed strongly, rather than as the settling proof, because a documentary built around scandal is structurally guaranteed to find and emphasize exactly the thing it's looking for. At this point I think we've thoroughly mapped both sides of this—you've got family history, incentive logic, an insider's testimony, and pattern-matching from experience, all pointing the same direction; I've raised base-rate and selection-bias counterpoints each time. That's a real disagreement about how much weight to put on vivid, memorable cases versus average cases, and I don't think one more round changes either position. Want to actually dig into the specific politician you started with?
